{"name":"Bahia Grass","hex":"#c4c5ad","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bahia grass","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of bahiagrass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bahia_grass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Paspalum notatum","blocks":[{"text":"Paspalum notatum, known commonly as bahiagrass, common bahia, and Pensacola bahia, is a tropical to subtropical perennial grass. It is known for its prominent V-shaped inflorescence consisting of two spike-like racemes containing multiple tiny spikelets, each about 2.8–3.5 millimetres (0.11–0.14 in) long."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Paspalum_notatum.jpg/330px-Paspalum_notatum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paspalum_notatum","wikidata":"Q1010817"},{"title":"Bahia","blocks":[{"text":"Bahia is one of the 26 states of Brazil, located in the Northeast Region of the country. It is the fourth-largest Brazilian state by population and the 5th-largest by area. Bahia's capital is the city of Salvador, on a spit of land separating the Bay of All Saints from the Atlantic. Once a stronghold of supporters of direct rule of Brazil by the Portuguese monarchy, and dominated by agricultural, slaving, and ranching interests, Bahia is now a predominantly working-class industrial and agricultural state. The state is home to 7% of the Brazilian population and produces 4.2% of the country's GDP. It is divided into 417 municipalities."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Bandeira_da_Bahia.svg/330px-Bandeira_da_Bahia.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bahia","wikidata":"Q40430"},{"title":"Grass","blocks":[{"text":"Grass refers to various families of plants. The three major families of grasslike plants are true grasses (Poaceae), sedges (Cyperaceae), and rushes (Juncaceae). Lawns and pasturelands are typically composed of true grasses, five of which cover 46% of the world's arable land: rice, wheat, maize, barley, and sugar cane."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Poa_annua.jpg/330px-Poa_annua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grass","wikidata":"Q643352"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Global_Forest_Change_tree-cover_loss_year_in_Bahia%2C_2001-2024.png/500px-Global_Forest_Change_tree-cover_loss_year_in_Bahia%2C_2001-2024.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Global_Forest_Change_tree-cover_loss_year_in_Bahia%2C_2001-2024.png/500px-Global_Forest_Change_tree-cover_loss_year_in_Bahia%2C_2001-2024.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tree-cover loss year in Bahia, 2001-2024, from the Global Forest Change dataset.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGlobal_Forest_Change_tree-cover_loss_year_in_Bahia%2C_2001-2024.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Belezas_da_Chapada_Diamantina_13.JPG/500px-Belezas_da_Chapada_Diamantina_13.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Belezas_da_Chapada_Diamantina_13.JPG/1280px-Belezas_da_Chapada_Diamantina_13.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Belezas_da_Chapada_Diamantina_13.JPG/500px-Belezas_da_Chapada_Diamantina_13.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chapada Diamantina National Park","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABelezas_da_Chapada_Diamantina_13.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.o